  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Isolated to scattered snow/rain showers will \noccur through the weekend. The better chances for showers will \noccur across northern and northwestern areas along with the \nwest-central highlands. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nA closed upper low will move south across the region today,\nexiting tonight. An upper level disturbance then moves south \nacross the region Sunday. Both the closed low and upper \ndisturbance will support isolated to scattered snow/rain \nshowers today and Sunday. The showers will generally be most \nnumerous across northern and northwestern areas, along with the \nwest-central highlands. Any snow accumulations today through \nSunday will be minimal, generally only a few tenths of an inch. \nHowever, local accumulations up to around an inch could occur \nacross mostly higher terrain areas.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Persistent dry conditions next week will lead \nto a worsening of the ongoing water resource drought impacting \nEastern and Northern Maine.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nNorthern stream ridging builds in Monday, then exits Monday\nnight, with associated subsidence keeping things dry. A northern\nstream shortwave crosses the area Tuesday, but with dry low\nlevels, it should go by precipitation free. Deep layered ridging\nthen builds in Tuesday night-Thursday, with associated\nsubsidence keeping things dry. The models then differ on how \nquickly the ridge axis exits to the east, allowing for a \nnorthern stream shortwave to cross the region in its wake. This \nresults in the timing of light precipitation across the North \nranging from Thursday night to next weekend. Regardless, the \namount of precipitation expected from the passing shortwave \nshould have no impact on existing drought conditions.\n\nThis continued dry weather will allow for soil moistures to\ndry/remain at much below normal levels next week.",
